# tools/ales_stereo_width_witness.rish — stereo_width: the image narrowed or widened by scaling the side and recombining, the first rung to spend ALES210's mid/side primitive (ALES211).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES211.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-121345_fill-ales211-lotus-stereo-width.md
# Stands on: lotus/mid_side.rye (ALES210 — the reversible mid/side change of basis, encode and decode, spent whole), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip
# and its proven `gain`, the side scaled by a fraction and saturated once), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip and its balance invariant). A keeper
# mixing a stereo master reaches for WIDTH more than almost any knob: pull the image toward mono to sit a part back, or push it out past the speakers for
# air. Width is exactly "scale the side, recombine" — the MID is the mono core both channels share, the SIDE the stereo spread, so scaling the side alone
# widens or narrows without touching the centre.
#
# lotus/stereo_width.rye composes two proven stages with NO new audio arithmetic: encode to mid/side (ALES210), scale the side by width/den (ALES2's gain),
# decode back (ALES210). The three edges name the knob. THE UNITY EDGE: width == den is the mid/side round-trip, exact on a same-parity master (the original
# byte for byte). THE MONO COLLAPSE: width == 0 zeroes the side, so both channels become the shared mid = (l+r)/2, the image pulled fully to centre. THE
# WIDEN LAW: width == 2·den doubles the side, so the per-sample channel difference |L−R| grows, the image pushed outward (saturating at the rail). THE
# CENTRED-INVARIANT LAW: a centred master (l == r) has a zero side, so width leaves it untouched at any setting — a mono signal has no width to turn. THE
# BALANCE / FAULT LAW: both channels stay balanced, and a zero or over-bound width refuses BadWidth before any write, the master untouched.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_width_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-width: Language EN — stereo_width narrows or widens a stereo master by scaling the SIDE channel and recombining, the first rung to spend ALES210's mid/side primitive. A keeper reaches for width more than almost any knob in the field: pull the image in toward mono so a part sits back in the mix, or push it out past the speakers for air. Width is exactly 'scale the side, recombine' — the MID is the mono core both channels share, the SIDE is the stereo spread, so scaling the side alone turns the width without touching what the channels have in common. This proves the mid/side primitive was worth laying first: width invents nothing, it composes."
say "ales-stereo-width: Style Radiant — the shape is stated positively as a composition of two proven stages with no new audio arithmetic: encode the master to mid/side in place (ALES210), scale the side by width/den through ALES2's proven gain (which saturates once so a wide setting pins at the rail rather than wrapping), decode back in place (ALES210). The three edges name the knob honestly. width == den is the mid/side round-trip — the near-identity, exact on a same-parity master. width == 0 is the mono collapse: the side is zeroed, so both channels become the shared mid = (l+r)/2, the image fully centred. width past den widens: the side grows, so the channel difference grows, the image pushed outward. A centred master (left == right) has a zero side, so width leaves it untouched at any setting — a mono signal has no width to turn. The width is validated before any write, so a bad fraction leaves the master untouched and balanced."
say "ales-stereo-width: Lens TAME — two bounded i16 Clips (left, right) transformed in place; the balance invariant is asserted on entry and exit, and the width fraction is bounded (max_width_den 4096, max_width_num 32768) and checked BEFORE encode ever runs, so a zero or over-bound width refuses BadWidth with the master untouched (the atomicity crux — the precheck precedes the first write); once the width is legal the gain over the full valid span and the decode cannot fault, so the op refuses whole up front or completes whole. It reuses ALES210's encode and decode (each total, void, balance-preserving) and ALES2's gain (the side scaled in i64 and saturated once) over their public APIs, inventing no new primitive; the unity, collapse, and widen laws are proven byte for byte against hand vectors on a same-parity master so the round-trip is exact, the widen law also checked by |L−R| growing per sample, and the centred-invariant law proven at both the collapse and 2× settings; siloed to lotus/, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_width"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_width.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-width: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_width.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-width: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-width: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-width' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-width: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-width line"
